 "note": "Malaysia's largest banks are Maybank, CIMB and Public Bank. Public Bank's card pages link no fee schedule this research could find (2026-10-30), so Maybank and CIMB are written. Maybank's credit card fees and charges page (read again 2026-10-30): every credit card's principal annual fee, with its first-year (or, for the Mercedes-Benz card, first-two-year) waiver and the yearly spending that waives the next year (written; the Visa Classic this file has held since 2026-10-29 is kept at 60 ringgit); the cards with a lifetime waiver cost nothing; the three American Express charge cards are paid in full each month (the Gold Card 238 ringgit, its first three years free; the Platinum Card 3,250). Finance charges on purchases are 15%, 17% or 18% a year by the holder's prompt payments in the last twelve months (12, at least 10, fewer), written as 15% to 18%; cash advances 18% a year and a fee of 2.52%, at least 11.65 ringgit (from 7 January 2026); the minimum payment 5% of the balance or 25 ringgit, whichever is higher; a late payment 1% of the unpaid outstanding balance, at least 10 and at most 100 ringgit (written as a share of the unpaid part); spending in another currency 2.25% on Visa and Mastercard (the network's 1.25% and Maybank's 1%) and a 2.5% conversion factor on American Express cards. The Islamic cards' management charges are the same figures and are written as the conventional ones. The RM25 service tax per card isn't written, the page not saying how often it falls; the Touch 'n Go Zing card's 10-ringgit subscription fee isn't written, the page not saying how often it's charged. No grace period is stated. CIMB's credit card fees and charges page (read 2026-10-30): the principal annual fee is waived on every personal card but the Travel World Elite (1,215.09 ringgit), the Travel World (554.72) and the e Credit Card (80; its waiver for 2026 and on spending from 2027 is in a notice not read and isn't written); a service tax of 25 ringgit on each card at activation and each annual renewal, written as a yearly fee; finance charges 15%, 17% or 18% a year by prompt payment, written as 15% to 18%; cash 18% a year and a cash advance fee of 1.0%, at least 4.11 ringgit; the minimum payment 5% of the current balance (with taxes, instalments and any unpaid minimum added) or 50 ringgit, whichever is higher (written as 5% with a 50 floor); interest-free 20 days from the statement date when paid in full; a late payment 1% of the unpaid balance, at least 10 and at most 100 ringgit; spending in another currency an administrative 1% (not on the Travel World Elite) plus the network's own fee, which the page doesn't state; only the 1% is written. The business cards aren't written. Wave 4 (2026-10-30): Hong Leong Bank, among Malaysia's largest banks, is added in Public Bank's place (Public Bank's rates-and-charges page links no card fee schedule; RHB's site answers Cloudflare's block, 2026-10-30). Hong Leong's credit card fees and charges page (read 2026-10-30): the principal annual fee, HLB Opus 3,000 ringgit, Visa Infinite P and Visa Infinite none, Visa WISE Gold 98, Visa Sutera Platinum 120, Visa Essential 100, Visa GSC Gold 150, Visa GSC Platinum 300, Visa I'm none (the Visa Infinite Doctor's Edition, open only to doctors, isn't written); retail interest 15%, 17% or 18% a year by prompt payment, written as 15% to 18%; cash advances 18% a year and a fee of 5% or 20 ringgit, whichever is higher; a late payment 1% of the total outstanding balance, at least 10 and at most 100 ringgit; 20 days free of interest from the statement date when the previous balance is paid in full; spending in another currency the network's 1% and the bank's 1% mark-up, written as 2% (1% on the Visa I'm, which carries no mark-up). The 25-ringgit service tax per card isn't written, the page not saying how often it falls; nor is the 8% tax on some fees, the paper-statement fee or the Zing card's fees. The page states no minimum payment.",
